'\" t
.\" helix-agent(1), helixdesktop/spec contracts/agent-access/cli.md
.TH HELIX-AGENT 1 "2026-11" "Helix" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
helix-agent \- let an agent read and act on your Helix session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B helix-agent
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B helix-agent
is the command-line front end to Helix's Agent Access, for agents such as
coding assistants and background automations. It is a thin client of the
shell's D-Bus interface
.B org.helixdesktop.Agent1
and has no policy of its own: the shell decides what an agent may do.
.PP
Agent access is off by default. The user turns it on and chooses how much
agents may do in
.B Settings \[u203A] Privacy & Security \[u203A] Agent Access.
The first time an agent asks for access in a session, the shell asks the
user (\fIDeny\fR, \fIAllow for this session\fR, or \fIAlways allow\fR).
While an agent is active, the top bar shows an indicator whose menu can
stop it.
.PP
The agent is the program that runs
.BR helix-agent ,
found by the shell through the process tree (shells and
.B env
in between are skipped). Set
.B HELIX_AGENT_NAME
to the name the user should see; it is shown, never trusted for identity.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
JSON output: one JSON object on standard output. The default when standard
output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Readable text. The default on a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ask
Never make the shell ask the user; where it would have to, fail (exit 12 or 18).
.TP
.B \-\-sensitive
Mark this action sensitive: with \fIConfirm sensitive actions\fR on, the user
confirms it first.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
How long to wait for the shell's answer (default 30; 0: no limit). Waits and
questions have their own \fB\-\-timeout\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help for the command (every command and subcommand has it).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The CLI's version and the shell's Agent Access version.
.SH COMMANDS
Each command needs an access level: \fIRead only\fR (read-only),
\fIControl the desktop\fR (desktop), \fIControl apps\fR (apps), or
\fIScreen and input\fR (screen-and-input); each includes the ones before it.
.SS Status
.TP
.B status
Whether agent access is on and what this agent may do. Never asks the user.
.TP
.BR request " [" \-\-level
.IR LEVEL ]
Ask the user for access now (default: the level set in Settings).
.SS "Read (Read only)"
.TP
.B state
The focused window, the workspace and quick settings.
.TP
.BR windows " [" \-\-app
.IR APP_ID "] [" \fB\-\-focused\fR ]
Open windows, with the document each shows where its app tells (a media
player's MPRIS URL, or the recent file whose name is in the title).
Windows of excluded apps and of Settings are left out.
.TP
.BR apps " [" \-\-running ]
Installed and running apps.
.TP
.BR workspaces ", " monitors
Workspaces; monitors.
.TP
.BR notifications " [" \-\-limit
.IR N ]
Recent notifications, newest first (at most 50). Their titles and bodies
only with \fINotifications access\fR; otherwise only each one's app and
time.
.TP
.B files recent \fR[\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-type \fIMIME\fR]
Recently used files (\fIrecently-used.xbel\fR), newest first; files only
excluded apps used are left out. \fIMIME\fR may be \fBimage/*\fR.
.TP
.B files search \fIQUERY\fR [\fB\-\-since \fIWHEN\fR] [\fB\-\-until \fIWHEN\fR] [\fB\-\-type \fIMIME\fR] [\fB\-\-in \fIFOLDER\fR] [\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR]
Search for files below \fIFOLDER\fR (default: the home folder) whose names
or contents have words starting with those of \fIQUERY\fR: through
LocalSearch when it runs, else by looking through the folder (names, and
small text files' contents). \fB\-\-since\fR and \fB\-\-until\fR are
compared with the time each file was last changed. \fB\-\-limit\fR: at
most 200 (default 50) for both \fBfiles\fR commands.
.TP
.B wait window \fR[\fB\-\-app \fIAPP_ID\fR] [\fB\-\-title \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-focused\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR]
Wait until a matching window is open (at once if one is), and print it.
\fITEXT\fR is matched anywhere in the title, ignoring case.
\fB\-\-timeout\fR: default 60, \fB0\fR waits as long as it takes; exit 21
when it runs out.
.TP
.B wait closed \fIWINDOW\fR [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR]
Wait until a window is closed (exit 22 if there is no such window).
.SS "Talk to the user (Read only)"
.TP
.B ask \fIQUESTION\fR [\fB\-\-choice \fITEXT\fR]... [\fB\-\-default \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR]
Ask the user a question in a dialog that names the agent and its program,
and print the answer: the text typed, or with \fB\-\-choice\fR (2 to 8,
each at most 64 characters) the choice picked. \fB\-\-default\fR fills in
the answer, or picks a choice first. The question is plain text of at most
500 characters. Exits 20 when the user cancels, 21 when \fB\-\-timeout\fR
(default 300 seconds, \fB0\fR: no limit) runs out. Only the user at the
machine can answer: input from remote desktop sessions and synthetic input
do not reach the dialog. One question of an agent's is shown at a time;
others wait. Locking the screen cancels it.
.TP
.B confirm \fIQUESTION\fR [\fB\-\-yes \fILABEL\fR] [\fB\-\-no \fILABEL\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR]
Ask the user yes or no, as \fBask\fR does; the buttons' labels are at
most 24 characters. Exits 0 for yes and 20 for no (with
\fB{"yes": false}\fR) or when cancelled.
.TP
.B remind add \fIWHEN TEXT\fR
Remind the user at \fIWHEN\fR (up to 366 days ahead) with a notification
under the agent's name; \fITEXT\fR is plain text of at most 500
characters. The user sees that it was set and can cancel it there.
Reminders are kept when the session ends; an agent may have 20 waiting.
.TP
.B remind list
This agent's reminders not yet shown.
.TP
.B remind cancel \fIID\fR
Cancel one of this agent's reminders (exit 22 if it has no such reminder).
.SS "Act (Control the desktop)"
.TP
.B open \fIURI-OR-PATH\fR [\fB\-\-app \fIAPP_ID\fR]
Open a file or folder in its default app (or \fIAPP_ID\fR), as a
double-click on the desktop does: a \fI.desktop\fR file starts its app only
when it is trusted (\fIAllow Launching\fR). Addresses: \fBhttp\fR,
\fBhttps\fR and \fBmailto\fR only. Relative paths are made absolute.
.TP
.B launch \fIAPP_ID\fR [\fIURI-OR-PATH\fR...]
Start an app the app grid shows, with files or addresses to open.
Excluded apps and Settings cannot be opened or launched (exit 14), also
not as the app that would open a file.
.TP
.B window \fIACTION WINDOW\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
\fBfocus\fR, \fBclose\fR (sensitive), \fBminimize\fR, \fBmaximize\fR,
\fBunmaximize\fR, \fBrestore\fR, \fBfullscreen\fR, \fBunfullscreen\fR,
\fBtile-left\fR, \fBtile-right\fR, \fBmove \fIX Y\fR,
\fBresize \fIWIDTH HEIGHT\fR, \fBto-workspace \fIINDEX\fR. Returns once
the windows show it (at most two seconds), so \fBwindows\fR shows the
result. Windows of excluded apps and of Settings are refused (exit 14).
.TP
.B workspace switch \fIINDEX\fR
Switch to a workspace (from 0).
.TP
.B toggle \fINAME\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR]
\fBdark-style\fR, \fBdo-not-disturb\fR, \fBnight-light\fR, \fBwifi\fR
(sensitive), \fBairplane-mode\fR (sensitive), \fBmute\fR; without on or
off it flips. Exit 23 for what this computer does not have.
.TP
.B set \fINAME VALUE\fR
\fBvolume\fR or \fBbrightness\fR, as a percentage (volume up to 150 with
over-amplification on). Setting the volume unmutes.
.TP
.B notify \fITITLE\fR [\fIBODY\fR] [\fB\-\-icon \fIICON\fR] [\fB\-\-urgency low\fR|\fBnormal\fR]
Show the user a notification, under \fIAgent:\fR and this agent's name;
plain text, at most 5 a minute. Prints its id.
.SS "Clipboard (Clipboard access)"
These need \fIClipboard access\fR on in Settings (exit 19 otherwise):
\fBget\fR and \fBtypes\fR the Read only level, \fBset\fR Control the
desktop. The shell reads and sets the clipboard for the agent; the
primary selection (middle-click paste) is never used. Reading is refused
(exit 14) when the shell, Settings, a password prompt or an excluded app
copied it, when a password manager marked it secret, while a system
prompt is shown, and while the screen is locked (exit 15). The activity
log records only the kind of content and its length.
.TP
.B clipboard get \fR[\fB\-\-type \fIMIME\fR] [\fB\-\-output \fIFILE\fR]
Print the clipboard: as text unless \fB\-\-type\fR asks for another
type (\fBtext/html\fR, \fBimage/png\fR, \fBimage/*\fR for any picture);
without text, a picture, else the first type offered. Text output is the
content as it is, without a line break added; binary content is written
only to a file (\fB\-\-output\fR) or a pipe. JSON output is the Clipboard
record. At most 16 MiB.
.TP
.B clipboard types
The types the clipboard offers, one per line.
.TP
.B clipboard set \fR[\fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-type \fIMIME\fR]
Put \fITEXT\fR, or standard input without it, on the clipboard as
\fB\-\-type\fR: \fBtext/plain\fR (the default), \fBtext/html\fR,
\fBtext/uri-list\fR or \fBimage/png\fR; at most 16 MiB. The shell holds
it until something else is copied.
.SS "Apps (Control apps)"
The shell reads and uses the window's controls through its accessibility
tree; \fBhelix-agent\fR never talks to the accessibility bus.
Password fields have no value and cannot be used (exit 14); neither can
windows of excluded apps, Settings, the shell's own prompts, or any window
while a system prompt (a password or permission prompt) is shown.
What is typed in text fields is not given unless \fIRead text fields\fR
is on in Settings: such fields show \fB(hidden)\fR (value-hidden: true)
instead of their text. Labels, other text, and the numbers of sliders and
spin buttons are given either way, and fields can still be filled in.
.TP
.B a11y tree \fIWINDOW\fR [\fB\-\-depth \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-node \fINODE\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-nodes \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-bounds\fR]
The window's controls as a tree (at most 1000 nodes unless
\fB\-\-max\-nodes\fR says more, up to 5000). Each node has an id for
the commands below; ids name the same control as long as the app keeps it.
\fB\-\-bounds\fR adds where each control is in its window.
.TP
.B a11y find \fIWINDOW\fR [\fB\-\-role \fIROLE\fR] [\fB\-\-name \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-state \fISTATE\fR]... [\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR]
Controls of a role (\fBbutton\fR, \fBtext\fR, \fBcheck box\fR…), whose
name contains \fITEXT\fR (ignoring case), in every \fISTATE\fR given.
.TP
.B a11y wait \fIWINDOW\fR [\fB\-\-role \fIROLE\fR] [\fB\-\-name \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-state \fISTATE\fR]... [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR]
Wait until such a control is there (default 60 seconds; 0: no limit);
prints it.
.TP
.B a11y do \fIWINDOW NODE ACTION\fR; \fBa11y press\fR|\fBfocus\fR|\fBselect \fIWINDOW NODE\fR
Use a control: one of its actions (as \fBa11y tree\fR lists them),
its first (\fBpress\fR), the keyboard focus (\fBfocus\fR; GTK 4 apps
cannot be given it this way, exit 23), or selecting it in its list or
tabs (\fBselect\fR). Actions that use the clipboard need \fIClipboard
access\fR (exit 19). Using a control whose name says it deletes, sends,
pays, removes or the like, and closing the window, is sensitive.
.TP
.B a11y set \fIWINDOW NODE VALUE\fR [\fB\-\-number\fR]
Replace an editable field's text, or set a slider's or spin button's
number (\fB\-\-number\fR, or text that is one); also with \fIRead text
fields\fR off. The log records the text's length only.
.SS "Screen and input (Screen and input)"
The riskiest level: Settings offers it separately, behind a warning.
While an agent takes a screenshot or sends input, and for 3 seconds
after, the top bar's indicator says an agent is controlling the screen.
Nothing works while the screen is locked (exit 15) or a system prompt (a
password or permission prompt) is shown (exit 14).
.TP
.B screenshot \fR[\fB\-\-window \fIWINDOW\fR | \fB\-\-monitor \fICONNECTOR\fR] [\fB\-\-area \fIX\fB,\fIY\fB,\fIWIDTH\fB,\fIHEIGHT\fR] [\fB\-\-output \fIFILE\fR]
Take a screenshot of every monitor, one monitor (its connector, as
\fBmonitors\fR lists them), or one window (whether or not it is shown),
or an area of it (logical pixels). The compositor draws it: excluded
apps' windows, Settings, password and permission prompts, the shell's
dialogs, notifications, overview, launcher, menus and on-screen keyboard,
and windows of programs that show any of those, are opaque black; the
pointer is not drawn. A window screenshot of such a window is refused
(exit 14). Password fields show what the app shows (dots). Writes the
PNG to \fIFILE\fR, or to standard output when it is not a terminal;
\fB\-\-json\fR gives it in base64. At most 16384 pixels either way.
The log records the size and how much was blacked out, never the
picture.
.TP
.B input key \fIKEYS\fR... [\fB\-\-window \fIWINDOW\fR]
Press key combinations in turn: \fBctrl+s\fR, \fBalt+F4\fR,
\fBReturn\fR, \fBshift+Tab\fR (XKB key names; modifiers \fBctrl\fR,
\fBalt\fR, \fBshift\fR, \fBsuper\fR, \fBaltgr\fR).
.TP
.B input type \fITEXT\fR [\fB\-\-window \fIWINDOW\fR]
Type \fITEXT\fR: any characters, whatever the keyboard layout (a line
break is Return); at most 10000.
.TP
.B input click \fIX Y\fR [\fB\-\-button left\fR|\fBmiddle\fR|\fBright\fR|\fBback\fR|\fBforward\fR] [\fB\-\-count \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-window \fIWINDOW\fR]
Click (\fIN\fR times, at most 3) at \fIX\fR, \fIY\fR in the window.
.TP
.B input move \fIX Y\fR; \fBinput scroll \fIDX DY\fR [\fB\-\-at \fIX\fB,\fIY\fR]; \fBinput drag \fIX1 Y1 X2 Y2\fR [\fB\-\-button \fIBUTTON\fR]
Move the pointer; scroll by wheel steps (negative: left, up) where the
pointer is or at \fIX\fR, \fIY\fR; drag with a button held.
.TP
.B input sequence \fISTEP\fR... [\fB\-\-window \fIWINDOW\fR]
Several steps in one go: \fBmove \fIX Y\fR, \fBclick \fR[\fIBUTTON\fR] [\fIX Y\fR],
\fBdouble-click \fR[\fIBUTTON\fR] [\fIX Y\fR], \fBmouse-down \fR[\fIBUTTON\fR] [\fIX Y\fR],
\fBmouse-up \fR[\fIBUTTON\fR], \fBkey \fIKEYS\fR, \fBkey-down \fIKEY\fR,
\fBkey-up \fIKEY\fR, \fBscroll \fIDX DY\fR, \fBtype \fITEXT\fR, \fBwait \fIMS\fR
(e.g. \fBkey-down shift click 10 20 key-up shift\fR). Keys and buttons
still held are let go at the end.
.PP
Input goes to one window: \fB\-\-window\fR (after the subcommand) names
it and it is focused first; otherwise the focused window. \fIX\fR and
\fIY\fR are logical pixels from the window's top-left corner. Not into
excluded apps, Settings, password or permission prompts, the shell's top
bar, dock, overview or notifications (exit 14). The compositor sends each
event straight to the app, never to its own shortcuts (Super, Alt+Tab,
Ctrl+Alt+F\fIn\fR and the lock key do nothing but reach the app), and
checks before each that the window still has the focus and the point is
on it: when another window, a dialog or a prompt takes the focus, the
rest is not sent (exit 1 or 14). Typing into a password field (as the app
marks it) is refused (exit 14).
.PP
\fBThe user's own input stops it.\fR Pressing a key or button, moving
or scrolling the mouse or touching the screen while input is being sent
stops it at once (exit 20, \fIStopped: the user took over the keyboard
or pointer\fR); for 2 seconds after, input is refused (exit 20). The
indicator's \fIStop\fR, and Settings, stop it at once too (exit 20).
Input is sensitive only when marked (\fB\-\-sensitive\fR). The log
records counts of moves, clicks, keys and shortcuts and the text's
length, never the text, keys or positions.
.SS Other
.TP
.B log \fR[\fB\-\-agent \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-since \fIWHEN\fR]
Show what agents did (reads the activity log; needs no access).
.TP
.B help \fR[\fICOMMAND\fR | \fBexit-codes\fR]
Help for a command, or the exit codes.
.TP
.B completions bash\fR|\fBzsh\fR|\fBfish
Print a shell completion script.
.PP
\fIWINDOW\fR is a window id from \fBwindows\fR. \fIWHEN\fR is an ISO 8601
date or date and time (local time unless it has an offset), or a relative
time: \fB+10m\fR, \fB+2h\fR, \fB\-1d\fR, \fByesterday\fR, \fBtoday\fR.
.PP
\fISensitive\fR actions (and any command run with \fB\-\-sensitive\fR) are
confirmed by the user first when \fIConfirm sensitive actions\fR is on; with
\fB\-\-no\-ask\fR they fail (exit 18), and a declined one exits 13.
.SH OUTPUT
With \fB\-\-json\fR, success prints one JSON object: the status dictionary
for \fBstatus\fR and \fBrequest\fR, \fB{"windows": [...]}\fR and similar for
lists, the record itself for single records, \fB{"id": N}\fR for
\fBnotify\fR, \fB{"name": "...", "on": true}\fR for \fBtoggle\fR,
\fB{"answer": "...", "choice": N}\fR for \fBask\fR (\fIN\fR is the choice's
index from 0, \-1 for a typed answer), \fB{"yes": true}\fR for
\fBconfirm\fR, \fB{"id": "..."}\fR for \fBremind add\fR, and
\fB{}\fR for commands without output. Failures print
.PP
.nf
{"error": {"code": "access-off", "exit-code": 10, "message": "...", "dbus-error": "..."}}
.fi
.PP
on standard output and nothing on standard error. Text output prints the
message on standard error.
.SH RECORDS
Records are the shell's dictionaries with the same keys; keys not listed
here may be added.
.TP
.B Window
id, app-id, title, workspace, all-workspaces, monitor, focused, x, y, width,
height, state (normal, maximized, fullscreen, minimized, tiled-left,
tiled-right), document (only when the app tells it).
.TP
.B App
id, name, running, windows.
.TP
.B Workspace
index, active, windows.
.TP
.B Monitor
connector, name, x, y, width, height, scale, primary.
.TP
.B State
focused-window, active-workspace, dark-style, do-not-disturb, night-light,
wifi, airplane-mode, muted, volume, brightness (wifi and airplane-mode
without NetworkManager, volume and muted without a sound server, and
brightness without a controllable screen are left out).
.TP
.B Notification
id, app-id, app-name, time, and summary and body, or redacted (true)
without \fINotifications access\fR.
.TP
.B File
uri, name, mime-type, modified, size (local files), used (recent files:
when last used).
.TP
.B Reminder
id, time, text.
.TP
.B Accessible node
id, role, name, description, states, value (text, or a number with min and
max; never for password fields, which have password: true instead, nor
for text fields while \fIRead text fields\fR is off, which have
value-hidden: true instead), value-truncated, actions, child-count, truncated (some children left out),
x, y, width, height (with \fB\-\-bounds\fR), children (\fBa11y tree\fR).
\fBa11y tree\fR prints {"window", "app-id", "truncated", "root"};
\fBa11y find\fR {"nodes": [...]}; \fBa11y wait\fR the node.
.TP
.B Clipboard
mime-type, and text (text types) or data (base64), or path with
\fB\-\-output\fR; types (every type offered).
.TP
.B Image
png (base64) or path, width, height (pixels), scale, x, y,
logical-width, logical-height (the rectangle captured, in logical
pixels), filled (how many windows and surfaces were blacked out).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TS
l l l.
0	\-	success
1	failed	anything else
2	usage	bad arguments
3	no-shell	the Helix shell cannot be reached
10	access-off	agent access is turned off
11	level-too-low	the access level in Settings is too low
12	not-granted	not allowed, and the user could not be asked
13	denied	the user denied this agent
14	excluded	the target is excluded
15	locked	the screen is locked
16	remote	access from remote sessions is off
17	sandboxed	sandboxed apps are refused
18	needs-confirmation	needs confirmation, but \-\-no\-ask
19	feature-off	clipboard access is off
20	no	the user said no, cancelled or stopped it
21	timed-out	timed out
22	not-found	no such window, app or item
23	not-supported	not supported by this session, computer or app
.TE
.PP
For exit 23 the message gives the shell's reason (in English) after
\fINot supported here:\fR, saying whether this session, this computer or
the app cannot do it (GTK 4 apps, for one, do not let the focus be moved
this way). A command this shell does not have at all says \fINot supported
by this Helix session\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HELIX_AGENT_NAME
The agent's display name (at most 64 characters).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where \fBlog\fR finds the activity log (\fIhelix/agent-activity/\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/helix/agent-activity/YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl
The activity log.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/helix/agent-grants.toml
Agents the user allowed always.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/helix/agent-reminders.json
Agents' reminders not yet shown.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ helix-agent status
$ HELIX_AGENT_NAME="Build bot" helix-agent request \-\-level read-only
$ helix-agent \-\-json windows \-\-app org.gnome.TextEditor
$ helix-agent wait window \-\-app org.gnome.Nautilus \-\-timeout 10
$ helix-agent files search pdf \-\-in ~/Downloads \-\-since yesterday \-\-type application/pdf
$ helix-agent \-\-json windows \-\-focused
$ helix-agent open ~/Downloads/report.pdf
$ helix-agent window tile-left 21
$ helix-agent workspace switch 1
$ helix-agent toggle dark-style on
$ helix-agent set volume 40
$ helix-agent notify "Build finished" "All 212 tests passed"
$ helix-agent ask "Which branch should I deploy?" \-\-choice main \-\-choice staging
$ helix-agent confirm "Delete the 3 old backups?" \-\-yes Delete \-\-no Keep
$ helix-agent remind add +30m "Check the deploy"
$ helix-agent a11y find 21 \-\-role button \-\-name save
$ helix-agent a11y press 21 3f2a9c1e_4b7d_4c1a_9e2f_0a6b5d8c7e31
$ helix-agent a11y set 21 8e1d4a6b_2c3f_4d5e_8a9b_1c2d3e4f5a6b "Quarterly report"
$ helix-agent screenshot \-\-output screen.png
$ helix-agent screenshot \-\-window 21 \-\-area 0,0,400,120 \-\-output top.png
$ helix-agent input click \-\-window 21 120 40
$ helix-agent input type "Quarterly report"
$ helix-agent input key ctrl+s
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gsettings (1),
.BR gdbus (1)
